WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:00.880
Vsauce!

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:04.480
Kevin here and you just lost a dollar.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:08.960
I mean not literally -- obviously this is
a fictional scenario that examines an unexpected

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:11.840
slice of numerical cognition.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:17.200
And in our scenario, a dollar is mysteriously
missing.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:22.800
You, and your friends Michael and Jake went
out to lunch and split the $30 bill evenly.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.960
You paid $10 bucks each.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:31.520
But the server accidentally overcharged you
and the bill should’ve been only $25 dollars.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:36.800
So she gives the busboy your $5 dollar refund
but he can’t figure out how to evenly divide

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.600
it amongst three people, and frankly, you
don’t even know that you've been overcharged,

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:47.120
so he just takes a $2 tip for his efforts
and gives you, Michael and Jake one dollar

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:48.160
back each.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.920
So now you’ve only paid 9 dollars each for
lunch.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:01:00.080
9 + 9 + 9 = 27 plus the busboy kept $2 for
himself for a total of $29.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:05.120
But you originally paid $30.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.160
So... a dollar is missing.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.320
How does a dollar disappear?

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:13.520
Where is the missing dollar?

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:20.000
Before we breakdown this riddle let’s try
this with different numbers and just see what

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:20.480
happens.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:25.360
Let’s say you still pay a $30 dollar bill
but this time the server realizes there’s

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:29.920
been a huge mistake, and the total should’ve
been only $10 bucks.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:37.360
So now the busboy gets $20 to return to you,
Michael and Jake, which he also can’t divide

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:37.920
equally.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:42.800
You don’t know about the exact price change,
so the busboy takes his $2 tip, leaving him

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:46.080
with $18 to cleanly divide three ways.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.560
He refunds each of you $6 bucks.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:52.320
Reducing your effective price to 4 dollars
each.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:57.840
So you paid $4 + $4 + $4 + plus the busboy
tip of $2 = $14.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:03.520
And just like that $16 is gone!

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:08.000
Because you only paid $14 for a $30 bill?

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:08.500
No.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:09.940
No!

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:11.280
No!

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:15.120
Something is clearly completely wrong with
this setup.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:18.400
This is actually a pretty popular and rather
old riddle.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:23.680
It seems to have originated in the mid 1700s
with mathematician Francis Walkingame’s

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:27.680
“Tutor’s Assistant,” a sort of arithmetic
textbook.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:34.320
"If 48 taken from 120 leaves 72, and 72 taken
from 91 leaves 19, and 7 taken from thence

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:39.760
leaves 12, what number is that, out of which,
when you have taken 48, 72, 19, and 7, leaves

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:40.260
12?"

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:51.040
Part of what makes the Missing Dollar Riddle
confusing is that it bombards you with numbers.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:56.160
That’s okay, though because your abilities
with numerical cognition are actually pretty

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:57.040
good.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:02.080
Yale cognitive scientist Karen Wynn found
that infants can understand simple arithmetic

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:03.120
like 1 + 1 = 2.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:11.680
If I bombard you with numbers, like 2 + 5
+ 3 + 1 + 7 + 12, you aren’t confused about

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:19.040
what’s happening and you can work through
it to an answer of 30.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:23.840
But when context is added to those numbers,
it’s easy to get sidetracked.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:29.120
We get so wrapped up in what’s happening
with the lunch, the refund, and the bus boy

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:37.840
that we gloss over the real math and hear
9 + 9 + 9 + 2 = 29 and wonder where the dollar

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:38.560
went?

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.640
But it’s not missing.

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:43.520
We just played a mathematical sleight of hand.

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:45.120
Here’s the breakdown.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:46.720
We started with $30.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:51.040
$25 of that is still in the restaurant’s
cash register.

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:57.120
The other $5 has been given back -- $3 have
been returned to you and $2 for the busboy’s

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:57.840
tip.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:03.600
25 + 3 + 2 = 30, so where’s the disconnect?

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:05.140
Really?

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:09.200
Where exactly &nbsp;is the disconnect?

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:10.480
Let’s find it.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:13.920
There are really two huge problems in this
riddle.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:19.680
One is how we think about that $3 refunded
dollars-- we’re only considering it once

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:22.640
when we really need to consider it twice.

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:28.480
And the second problem is how we’re incorrectly
factoring in the bus boy’s tip.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:30.400
Let’s talk about that tip first.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:37.040
Because the bus boy takes his $2 tip, that's
money that’s never returned to you, Michael

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:43.360
or Jake, and therefore must now be considered
as part of the total cost of the lunch.

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:49.040
Which means that the new cost of the meal
is the $25 still in the cash register, plus

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:52.640
the bus boy’s $2 tip equaling $27.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:57.760
When each of the three of you pays $10 bucks
and then gets $1 dollar back, your effective

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:03.120
cost of the meal -- the food plus the waiter’s
tip -- is $9 each, for $27.

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:07.120
That leaves $3 dollars missing.

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:11.280
The $3 dollars returned to you by the bus
boy.

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:14.640
So now we’re left with the $3 problem.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:21.440
The 9 plus 9 plus 9 plus 2 equation of the
riddle is simply the wrong equation.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:27.520
Actually, as we just learned, the 9’s are
right but the 2 is wrong.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:30.800
The 2 is already factored into the 9’s.

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:34.000
What we need to do is add back the 3.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:38.880
Which is kinda confusing and part of that
sleight of hand.

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:44.400
The riddle uses the 3 to subtract from the
customers’ new final, total cost of the

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:51.680
meal, but we have to add it back in at the
end to get to the original $30 that changed

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:52.480
hands.

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:54.640
Look.
You paid ten dollars each.

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:59.360
Refund your $3 taking the amount you paid
down to $27.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:04.480
And then give the busboy his $2 tip and the
restaurant keeps $25.

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:08.880
There is no missing dollar.

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:13.680
The riddle is just answering the right question
the wrong way.

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:20.560
Legendary statistician John Tukey once said,
“An approximate answer to the right question

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:25.120
is worth a great deal more than a precise
answer to the wrong question.”

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:32.240
When it comes to the Missing Dollar Riddle,
the precise answer to the right question is

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:36.160
worth at least... a buck.

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:47.840
And always -- thanks for watching.

